issue.body:
Hi,

I'm using a npm package that has lazi-req as a dependency. I'm using webpack to bundle my application.

The line:
```javascript
const lazyReq = require('lazy-req')(require);`
```
is bundled by webpack as the following:
```javascript
var lazyReq = __webpack_require__("./node_modules/bin-wrapper/node_modules/lazy-req/index.js")(!(function webpackMissingModule() {
    var e = new Error("Cannot find module \".\"");
    e.code = 'MODULE_NOT_FOUND';
    throw e;
}()));
```
As I understand, webpack replace the `require` function that should be passed to lazy-req and try to import a module named `.`, as no name is given. Obviously, it fails.

Is there a way to avoid that ?

Thank you

body:
So, after countless hours of investigation(and pounding my head against the keyboard in trial and error), it appears this was broken with the removal of dynamic require by default.

To bypass the issue, you need to set ```module.unknownContextRegExp``` and ```module.exprContextRegExp``` in your Webpack config to enable dynamic imports.

**webpack.config.babel.js**
```
export default {
    ...
    module: {
        exprContextRegExp: /^\.\/.*$/,
        unknownContextRegExp: /^\.\/.*$/,
        rules: [...]
    }
    ...
};
```

## Summary (For those who care)

**lib/WebpackOptionsDefaulter.js** v2+
```
this.set("module.exprContextRegExp", false);
this.set("module.unknownContextRegExp", false);
```

**lib/WebpackOptionsDefaulter.js** v1.15
```
this.set("module.exprContextRegExp",  /^\.\/.*$/);
this.set("module.unknownContextRegExp", /^\.\/.*$/);
```

These values are passed around in many places and used during dependency creation, which ultimately causes the ```require.resolve``` expression to yield no dependencies, thus falling into the following else block:

**lib/dependencies/ContextDependencyTemplateAsRequireCall.js**
```
const containsDeps = dep.module && dep.module.dependencies && dep.module.dependencies.length > 0;

const isAsync = dep.module && dep.module.async;
if(dep.module && (isAsync || containsDeps)) {
    if(dep.valueRange) {
        if(Array.isArray(dep.replaces)) {
            for(let i = 0; i < dep.replaces.length; i++) {
                const rep = dep.replaces[i];
                 source.replace(rep.range[0], rep.range[1] - 1, rep.value);
            }
        }
        source.replace(dep.valueRange[1], dep.range[1] - 1, ")");
        source.replace(dep.range[0], dep.valueRange[0] - 1, "__webpack_require__(" + comment + JSON.stringify(dep.module.id) + ")(" + (typeof dep.prepend === "string" ? JSON.stringify(dep.prepend) : "") + "");
    } else {
        source.replace(dep.range[0], dep.range[1] - 1, "__webpack_require__(" + comment + JSON.stringify(dep.module.id) + ")");
    }
} else {
    const content = require("./WebpackMissingModule").module(dep.request);
    source.replace(dep.range[0], dep.range[1] - 1, content);
}
```

Not sure exactly what the actual solution is at this point in time, but I managed to bypass this bug for now.

Nevertheless, I hope this helps someone.

¯\\_(ツ)_/¯
